      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
Your house currently has alot of air leakage that needs to be aggressively managed:

** There appears to be an old heat supply opening at the top of the foundation wall (above the stack of wood pellets) that was previously used to supply heat through the space below the stairs (in the corridor that adjoins the house &amp; garage) and into the garage. Connecting the forced air ductwork from a house to an adjoined garage does not meet code and it is good this ductwork has been removed. However ....... the old hole through the top of the foundation wall is not well sealed and allows ALOT of air leakage (heat loss) from the house.

** The top of the rear foundation wall (top, back/left corner) has various holes through the header space that need to be sealed.

** Around the attic hatch (this can be sealed with a removeable caulking such as "Draft Attack", that you can get from the local hardware store

** The 2 x co-vented 6" chimneys for the furnace and hot water, as well as the 6" combustion air supply pipe that brings in combustion air for these 2 appliances - these will all be eliminated if you upgrade them as outlined within this report.

** Exterior wall electrical outlets and switches. These can be draft proofed by installing foam backer pads that you can get from the local hardware store.

Savings: I have modeled a modest 14% reduction in overall air leakage (I hope you can achieve 40%) that could potentially save $ 227 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.5 GJ, and qualify for a rebate through the Canada Greener Homes Program.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the 14% reduction. Larger reductions of air leakage can increase this grant up to a maximum $1,300.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The exterior walls of your house are 2x4@16" with R12 insulation. As most new housing has a minimum of R30 walls, I thought I would model adding another 3" (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene foam board insulation to the full exterior of the house (includes the wall adjoining the entrance corridor) ........ including the estimated energy savings &amp; possible grants/rebates to help you decide.

The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program provides 3 tiers of exterior wall insulation rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($1,500 for adding R10 - R14, $2,500 for adding R15 - R19, and $4,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding. You have recently upgraded all of the windows &amp; doors in your home, so this upgrade is for your future planning.

Savings:
By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 582 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).

To qualify for an EEIP exterior wall insulation rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits

For more information regarding the rebate program requirements and application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,940 is available for adding R15 insulation to 100% of the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</mainWalls>
                <foundation>
The foundation walls of your house are concrete with interior framed/insulated wall, and the upper 3' of these walls (about 39% of the total foundation wall area) is above grade and has the same exterior siding as the upper main floor walls. As outlined in the previous wall insulation recommendation, if you proceed to add R15 to the exterior walls of the upper main floor ((includes the wall adjoining the entrance corridor), this would obviously be continued down over the top 3' of the "above grade" foundation walls. The 39% area mentioned includes the top 3' of the foundation wall adjoining the entrance corridor.

The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program provides 2 tiers of foundation wall insulation rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($625 for adding R10 - R23, $1,250 for adding R24+).

Savings:
By increasing 39% of the foundation walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 214 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 243 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).

To qualify for an EEIP foundation wall insulation rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits

For more information regarding the rebate program requirements and application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $532 is available for adding R15 insulation to 39% of the foundation wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the foundation wall area of your home must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</foundation>
                <windows>
Most of the windows in your house are older and some do not close well (ie: the bathroom window on the main floor), and you were interested in the estimated annual energy savings if they were all upgraded to new ENERGY STAR® qualified windows. If you proceed with this upgrade, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m²K. For your house, I modeled a more efficient window with a U-Value no higher than 1.05 W/m²K.

Savings:
By replacing the windows in your house with ones that meets the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 227 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,250 in rebates (10 windows @ $325 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage</windows>
                <doors>
You were wondering about replacing the 2 entrance doors to the house (front &amp; the one that connects from the kitchen to the hallway between the house &amp; garage) with new ENERGY STAR® qualified units, If you proceed with this upgrade, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.

Savings:
By replacing both entrance doors with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 11 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $325.00 in rebates (2 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 121 litre oil hot water tank, that vents up a 6" chimney - co-vented with the furnace.

As your house already has gas service lines inside the house, I have modeled the opportunity to replace the oil hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current oil hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.95 EF, you could potentially save $ 245 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house currently has an older 81% efficient oil furnace that vents up a 6" chimney - co-vented with the oil hot water tank. As your house already has gas service lines inside the house, I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1,425 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 29.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

PELLET STOVE HEAT SUPPLEMENT

You have a pellet stove in the lower level of your home that is currently used for a high percentage of the heating season, but it depends upon heat naturally rising and mixing throughout the house. We discussed an option for you to have the high efficiency ECM blower fan of the new furnace used to circulate your pellet stove heat. Many HVAC folks can set this up with 2 smart thermostats. The one on the upper floor is set to heating mode to kick on the furnace if the temp gets too low. The one in the basement is set to AC Mode where the set "upper temp" will be triggered by the pellet stove heat in the basement ......... turning on only the furnace fan to start circulating that pellet stove heat throughout the house !!

Savings:
For now, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to use the pellet stove to supplement up to 75% of the heating for the lower level of this house (as compared to the new 96% efficient furnace). The calculated net savings (reduced propane gas cost for the furnace, less cost of pellets) is about $900 per year (or about $2,325 per year when combined with the savings from the new furnace.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Renewable Energy Systems

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front (SE) facing roof, directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 3,716 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,204 and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>
Your house has two toilets and the one in the basement is an older toilet that can be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 1000 grams per flush !!

Savings:
By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:
https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 2 x bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.0 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace, hot water tank and wood pellet stove all draw their combustion air from inside the house and are susceptible to back-drafting combustion products into the house. All of my recommendations within this report focus on upgrades that will have these 3 appliances become direct-vented, each with their own dedicated outside combustion air supply.

During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in your home. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside
        - 2 x bathroom exhaust fans
        - Clothes dryer
        - There is a 5" fresh makeup air supply connected to the return air plenum of the furnace
             ** This fresh air supply should have an in-line damper so you can manually adjust air supply based on
                 your evaluation of the indoor air quality:
                     - too stale inside with excess moisture = open up the damper a bit more
                     - too dry and alot of statis electricity = close down the damper a bit more

If you choose to replace or install any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STAR® qualified units. For a new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Hay River

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:
Electricity - $17.77 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.35 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $1.32 per litre 
Propane - $0.82 per litre delivered, $3.78 per m3 piped to the house
Wood - $310.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $4.86 per bag, $258.57 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Contracted Senior Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
